        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            One coat for penetrating stains, two for solid stains and deck paints. The difference is where the product lives: a penetrating stain protects from inside the wood and is limited by how much the wood can hold, while a film former builds its protection in layers on the surface.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Where a second coat of penetrating stain is called for, it must go on wet-on-wet within the label's window, usually 30 to 60 minutes. A second coat the following day cannot penetrate the sealed surface and becomes the tacky, peeling film everyone is trying to avoid.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Let the deck decide rather than the can. Stain still sitting wet after fifteen minutes means stop and brush the surplus out; stain vanishing in three minutes means the wood will take more. And remember that coats are not the durability lever — pigment level and preparation do far more for how long the finish lasts.
          </p>
        </div>


